aeg-d-xcop/v2/m000082,aeg-s-000181,profile_point,dynamics,hydrostatic,"X-ray and SZ hydrostatic g_obs, non-parametric reconstruction, and g_bar from gas, central-galaxy and satellite masses, both as released by Eckert et al. 2022",g_obs,2777.270751953125,3d,2.8949703745340116e-11,6.68319002569695e-12,5.410353588752859e-12,5.0289069783437945e-12,8.495630685490668e-14,8.495630685490668e-14,,,,none,not_separable,absent,not_separable,aeg-d-xcop/v2/o000163,aeg-d-xcop/v2/o000164,none,1,default,,true,,xcop_hse_equilibrium;xcop_spherical;xray_hse_bias=0,xray_hse_bias,,#124,data_E22.tar.gz:A2142/gbar_gobs_A2142.fits:row 20,"stellar projection: undetermined (part A, A6); stellar normalisation against Ghizzardi et al. 2021: 2.27 (part A, A9)",,not_given_by_source,,not_given_by_source,6.68319002569695e-12,5.410353588752859e-12,,,4.51,0.03
aeg-d-xcop/v2/m000083,aeg-s-000183,profile_point,dynamics,hydrostatic,"X-ray and SZ hydrostatic g_obs, non-parametric reconstruction, and g_bar from gas, central-galaxy and satellite masses, both as released by Eckert et al. 2022",g_obs,90.88128662109375,3d,2.3251191250128755e-10,1.8541720242515325e-11,1.8766521323043683e-11,6.796421758714644e-11,5.8576385929265395e-12,5.8576385929265395e-12,,,,none,not_separable,absent,not_separable,aeg-d-xcop/v2/o000165,aeg-d-xcop/v2/o000166,none,1,default,,true,,xcop_hse_equilibrium;xcop_spherical;xray_hse_bias=0,xray_hse_bias,,#124,data_E22.tar.gz:A2319/gbar_gobs_A2319.fits:row 1,"Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""This effect is particularly striking in the case of A2319, for which HSE masses are known to be biased low \citep[see the detailed discussion in][]{ghirardini18}."" (Eckert et al. 2022, hydromass.tex l. 435) | Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""We found that deviations from HSE are low, at the level of $6-10\%$, with the notable exception of A2319. However, the stellar content had to be assumed."" (Eckert et al. 2022, hydromass.tex l. 435) | Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""We find similar results in this analysis, as described in Sect. \ref{sec:fbar}, where we can see that, with the exception of a single system that is known to be strongly biased \citep[A2319;][]{ghirardini18}, the measured baryon fractions agree with the expectations at $R_{500}$ and slightly exceed the cosmic baryon fraction beyond this point, indicating a mild level of hydrostatic bias."" (Eckert et al. 2022, hydromass.tex l. 502) | Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""Finally, with the exception of a system known for its high level of nonthermal pressure support (A2319), the total cumulative baryon budget (gas + stars) is close to the cosmic baryon fraction $\Omega_b/\Omega_m$, with a slight excess of 10-20\% that can most likely be attributed to a hydrostatic bias (see Sect. \ref{sec:fbar})."" (Eckert et al. 2022, hydromass.tex l. 512) | Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""With the exception of one peculiar system for which there is clear evidence of a breakdown of hydrostatic equilibrium \citep[A2319,][]{ghirardini18}, the comparison of HSE masses with other techniques \citep{ettori19} and the fitted gas and baryon fractions \citep{eckert19} indicate that the HSE assumption is valid at the $\lesssim20\%$ level out to $R_{200}$."" (Eckert et al. 2022b (Paper II), einasto.tex l. 80) | Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""We can see that all the quoted outliers exhibit values of $w$ greater than $0.02$, which shows that their X-ray morphology is unrelaxed. A number of possible issues may render our mass reconstruction unreliable in unrelaxed systems. First, the validity of the hydrostatic equilibrium assumption in unrelaxed systems is unclear, as these systems are likely undergoing merging events at the present time."" (Eckert et al. 2022b (Paper II), einasto.tex l. 161) | Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""The substantial intrinsic scatter, $\sigma_{\alpha}=0.14_{-0.05}^{+0.10}$, is dominated by a few outliers with $\alpha\gtrsim 0.5$, all of which appear morphologically disturbed, as indicated by a large centroid shift. The high Einasto indices are therefore likely a systematic effect in the reconstruction of the mass profiles, caused by hydrostatic bias and/or miscentering."" (Eckert et al. 2022b (Paper II), einasto.tex l. 227) | stellar projection: undetermined (part A, A6); stellar normalisation against Ghizzardi et al. 2021: 2.25 (part A, A9)",,not_given_by_source,,not_given_by_source,1.8541720242515325e-11,1.8766521323043683e-11,,,33.08,0.17
aeg-d-xcop/v2/m000084,aeg-s-000183,profile_point,dynamics,hydrostatic,"X-ray and SZ hydrostatic g_obs, non-parametric reconstruction, and g_bar from gas, central-galaxy and satellite masses, both as released by Eckert et al. 2022",g_obs,124.83446502685547,3d,2.0887240548272956e-10,1.3004133360416947e-11,1.1275193452509047e-11,4.5705411677587904e-11,3.448419284499127e-12,3.448419284499127e-12,,,,none,not_separable,absent,not_separable,aeg-d-xcop/v2/o000167,aeg-d-xcop/v2/o000168,none,1,default,,true,,xcop_hse_equilibrium;xcop_spherical;xray_hse_bias=0,xray_hse_bias,,#124,data_E22.tar.gz:A2319/gbar_gobs_A2319.fits:row 2,"Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""This effect is particularly striking in the case of A2319, for which HSE masses are known to be biased low \citep[see the detailed discussion in][]{ghirardini18}."" (Eckert et al. 2022, hydromass.tex l. 435) | Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""We found that deviations from HSE are low, at the level of $6-10\%$, with the notable exception of A2319. However, the stellar content had to be assumed."" (Eckert et al. 2022, hydromass.tex l. 435) | Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""We find similar results in this analysis, as described in Sect. \ref{sec:fbar}, where we can see that, with the exception of a single system that is known to be strongly biased \citep[A2319;][]{ghirardini18}, the measured baryon fractions agree with the expectations at $R_{500}$ and slightly exceed the cosmic baryon fraction beyond this point, indicating a mild level of hydrostatic bias."" (Eckert et al. 2022, hydromass.tex l. 502) | Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""Finally, with the exception of a system known for its high level of nonthermal pressure support (A2319), the total cumulative baryon budget (gas + stars) is close to the cosmic baryon fraction $\Omega_b/\Omega_m$, with a slight excess of 10-20\% that can most likely be attributed to a hydrostatic bias (see Sect. \ref{sec:fbar})."" (Eckert et al. 2022, hydromass.tex l. 512) | Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""With the exception of one peculiar system for which there is clear evidence of a breakdown of hydrostatic equilibrium \citep[A2319,][]{ghirardini18}, the comparison of HSE masses with other techniques \citep{ettori19} and the fitted gas and baryon fractions \citep{eckert19} indicate that the HSE assumption is valid at the $\lesssim20\%$ level out to $R_{200}$."" (Eckert et al. 2022b (Paper II), einasto.tex l. 80) | Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""We can see that all the quoted outliers exhibit values of $w$ greater than $0.02$, which shows that their X-ray morphology is unrelaxed. A number of possible issues may render our mass reconstruction unreliable in unrelaxed systems. First, the validity of the hydrostatic equilibrium assumption in unrelaxed systems is unclear, as these systems are likely undergoing merging events at the present time."" (Eckert et al. 2022b (Paper II), einasto.tex l. 161) | Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""The substantial intrinsic scatter, $\sigma_{\alpha}=0.14_{-0.05}^{+0.10}$, is dominated by a few outliers with $\alpha\gtrsim 0.5$, all of which appear morphologically disturbed, as indicated by a large centroid shift. The high Einasto indices are therefore likely a systematic effect in the reconstruction of the mass profiles, caused by hydrostatic bias and/or miscentering."" (Eckert et al. 2022b (Paper II), einasto.tex l. 227) | stellar projection: undetermined (part A, A6); stellar normalisation against Ghizzardi et al. 2021: 2.25 (part A, A9)",,not_given_by_source,,not_given_by_source,1.3004133360416947e-11,1.1275193452509047e-11,,,33.08,0.17
