mic baryon fraction $\Omega_b/\Omega_m$, with a slight excess of 10-20\% that can most likely be attributed to a hydrostatic bias (see Sect. \ref{sec:fbar})."" (Eckert et al. 2022, hydromass.tex l. 512) | Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""With the exception of one peculiar system for which there is clear evidence of a breakdown of hydrostatic equilibrium \citep[A2319,][]{ghirardini18}, the comparison of HSE masses with other techniques \citep{ettori19} and the fitted gas and baryon fractions \citep{eckert19} indicate that the HSE assumption is valid at the $\lesssim20\%$ level out to $R_{200}$."" (Eckert et al. 2022b (Paper II), einasto.tex l. 80) | Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""We can see that all the quoted outliers exhibit values of $w$ greater than $0.02$, which shows that their X-ray morphology is unrelaxed. A number of possible issues may render our mass reconstruction unreliable in unrelaxed systems. First, the validity of the hydrostatic equilibrium assumption in unrelaxed systems is unclear, as these systems are likely undergoing merging events at the present time."" (Eckert et al. 2022b (Paper II), einasto.tex l. 161) | Source statement on hydrostatic validity (part A §5; not an exclusion, part B rule 4): ""The substantial intrinsic scatter, $\sigma_{\alpha}=0.14_{-0.05}^{+0.10}$, is dominated by a few outliers with $\alpha\gtrsim 0.5$, all of which appear morphologically disturbed, as indicated by a large centroid shift. The high Einasto indices are therefore likely a systematic effect in the reconstruction of the mass profiles, caused by hydrostatic bias and/or miscentering."" (Eckert et al. 2022b (Paper II), einasto.tex l. 227) | stellar projection: undetermined (part A, A6); stellar normalisation against Ghizzardi et al. 2021: 2.25 (part A, A9)",,not_given_by_source,,not_given_by_source,5.756266123480014e-12,5.587965420245089e-12,,,33.08,0.17
